# .gitignore for cpu1's git-init'd /srv/ (repo root is /srv/, not /srv/docker/
# -- other /srv siblings exist: cron/, log.txt). Host-specific -- see
# gitignore-db1/gitignore-db2 for those hosts' separate copies; do not merge
# them, since a finding specific to one host can wrongly exclude something
# another host actually needs tracked, or leave a no-op pattern for
# something that never existed there. `git init` runs from /srv/ itself,
# not /srv/docker/.
#
# ---- scope: only docker/ is tracked, everything else at /srv root is ignored ----
# cron/ and log.txt (and anything else that shows up at the /srv root later)
# are out of scope for this repo entirely -- not audited, not reviewed for
# secrets, just excluded outright. Revisit deliberately if this repo's scope
# is ever meant to grow beyond docker/.
/*
!/docker/
!/.gitignore
!/.githooks/

# ---- everything below only ever applies inside docker/, since that's the only
#      top-level entry not excluded above ----
#
# This file holds only host-wide GENERIC rules (secret file patterns, runtime
# data, stale directories, nested repos). It deliberately does NOT list any
# specific per-service file -- those live in a per-directory .gitignore
# instead, one level down, right next to the file it excludes.
#
# sanitize_service_files.py --write generates each service's .gitignore for
# you automatically (only for services where it actually found something to
# redact) -- review its output and copy it to
# /srv/docker/<service>/.gitignore. This root file only needs manual edits
# for genuinely host-wide patterns or genuinely root-level (/docker/<file>)
# loose files.

# ---- secrets: never track ----
.env
.env.*
# .env.example is the deliberate exception: it's the git-trackable companion
# to .env (secrets blanked, config values kept as real values -- built by
# build_env_example.py from a service's real .env). ".env.*" above would
# otherwise silently exclude it too, since it also starts with ".env." --
# this exact gap was found and fixed on db1/db2, applying the fix here from
# the start rather than waiting to rediscover it.
!.env.example
*.env
*.env_*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
*.der
htpasswd
# unlike db1/db2 where htpasswd is a single file, cpu1's proxy/htpasswd is a
# DIRECTORY holding one htpasswd file per dagster environment -- the bare
# "htpasswd" pattern above only matches the file shape; this one covers the
# directory shape too.
htpasswd/
users.acl
ldap.toml

# cpu1-specific: proxy/CA.key is the actual CA private key that signs every
# other cert on this host -- already covered by the generic *.key pattern
# above, called out explicitly here because of how sensitive it is (this is
# not just "a service's key", it's the root of trust for this host's whole
# PKI). Confirm it's genuinely excluded before the first commit, don't just
# assume the generic pattern caught it.

# NOTE: .gitignore does NOT support trailing inline comments -- a "#"
#      after a pattern on the same line is parsed as part of the pattern
#      itself (making it match nothing), not stripped as a comment. Comments
#      must be on their own line, before the pattern.

# ---- loose top-level files at /srv/docker root, deferred for now (not yet
#      reviewed for secrets -- run_all.sh only iterates service directories,
#      so these were never scanned). ----
/docker/update_watchtower_images.py
/docker/watchtower_updates.txt

# ---- a huge, world-writable, duplicated binary tarball sitting loose at
#      /srv/docker root (18GB, mode 777) and again inside conda/ -- not
#      config, not something to ever track, and the permissions/duplication
#      are worth raising with whoever owns this host separately from the
#      git question. Bare filename (no leading slash) so it's caught in
#      both locations. ----
amfileon-conda.tar

# ---- runtime data / logs / backups: not config, don't track ----
*.log
*.rdb
data/
restored-data/
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2
*.tar.gz.base64

# ---- editor/OS artifacts: never intentional, safe to exclude anywhere ----
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# ---- large/misc runtime data directories found by direct review ----
# conda/redis-data/ holds a live dump.rdb (already caught by *.rdb above,
# excluding the whole directory too since more could land there later).
redis-data/

# ---- nested repos: managed by their own .git, not this one ----
# dockprom/ has its own .git (a customized clone of the upstream project,
# same as on db1/db2) -- excluded wholesale so the top-level repo doesn't
# treat it as an embedded repo/gitlink. Commit its local changes inside the
# directory itself: `cd docker/dockprom && git add/commit`.
dockprom/

# ---- live, dedicated secrets-management services: deliberately excluded
#      from the bulk/scripted pass entirely and handled by hand, as root,
#      when actually working on them -- but that only controls the
#      sanitizer, not git, so these need an explicit exclusion here too or
#      `git add -A` would stage their raw, never-scanned contents. ----
# vault-agent-example/ holds a live vault-token file despite the "example"
# name -- treat it as real until confirmed otherwise, same category as
# db1/db2's vault/vault2/infisical.
vault-agent-example/
vault/
vault2/
infisical/

# ---- conda/ is a dev/testing sandbox with a LOT of accumulated clutter:
#      8 different .env variants, a vault-token, a cacert.pem, many stray
#      backup/old compose files -- excluded WHOLESALE, same category as
#      vault/vault2/infisical below: needs a deliberate by-hand pass, not
#      blind bulk tracking. Important: being in run_all.sh's SKIP array
#      only stops the SANITIZER from scanning it -- it does NOT stop
#      `git add -A` from staging its raw, never-scanned contents. Learned
#      the hard way on cpu2 (same conda/ shape): the pre-commit hook's
#      key-name check caught real secret-shaped assignments in
#      conda/docker-compose.yaml and conda/entrypoint.sh on the very first
#      `git add -A`, because this entry was originally just a comment, not
#      an actual exclusion pattern. ----
conda/

# ---- stale/duplicate/abandoned files worth deleting outright, ignored here as a fallback ----
# clickhouse/docker-compose.yaml.save, clickhouse/keeper.xml.bak,
# conda/docker-compose.yaml.old, conda/Dockerfile.bak,
# conda/docker-compose.override.yaml.BAK, minio/docker-compose.yml.backup,
# watchtower/docker-compose.yml.bak, restic/docker-compose.bak,
# gitlab-runner/config.toml.bak -- all caught generically below; better to
# `rm` these than carry them forever, but not this tool's call to make.
# NOTE: gitignore patterns are case-sensitive on Linux -- bracket expressions
# below match any case, same lesson learned on db1/db2 (a real "CA.pem.BAK"
# was missed by a bare "*.bak" there).
*.[bB][aA][kK]
*.save
*.old
*.backup
*-old/
*_old/
*test/
*failed/
*.back.yaml
*.new.yaml

# ---- cert/key material directories: never track ----
certs/
certs_old/
pki/
tmp-conf/

# ---- everything else inside docker/ ----
# Any file not covered by a rule above (a clean docker-compose.yaml, nginx.conf,
# healthcheck.py, etc. that had nothing to redact) is tracked as its real,
# unmodified self by default. Per-service .gitignore files (one per service
# directory, generated by sanitize_service_files.py --write when it finds
# something) are what carve out the specific exceptions.
